Rem ==================================================================
Rem seednet.shoddy — reckoner seed: net.shoddy's request/response half
Rem
Rem NETGET fetches a URL from the prompt. NETREQUEST sends whatever you
Rem like and hands back the whole reply. NETALLOWED — seedbuiltin's, not
Rem this seed's — says whether either can work at all.
Rem
Rem ---- THREE OBJECTIONS, AND WHICH ONE WAS REAL ----------------------
Rem
Rem THE HANDLE. R3.5(b) keeps a socket off the stack, and this seed
Rem never puts one there: a request opens, talks and closes inside one
Rem word. Nothing to strand, so nothing to hold by name either — unlike
Rem a file or a window, a request/response has no life between lines,
Rem which is why this seed uses the resource table not at all.
Rem
Rem THE CAPABILITY, and this is the one the tree used to record as
Rem decisive: "--allow-net is not something a session can obtain half
Rem way through". True, and it does not follow that nothing can be
Rem bridged. A mill STARTED with the flag has the capability; what was
Rem missing was a way to ASK, since every socket builtin begins by
Rem aborting when the network is off. NETALLOWED is that question, and
Rem TRYTCPREQUEST reports the same fact as an ordinary refusal.
Rem
Rem THE ABORTS, which turned out to be the real obstacle and the largest
Rem one. TCPSEND and TCPRECV die eight ways between them on plain
Rem network faults, and TCPSECURE takes the handle with it on a failed
Rem handshake. A bridge built from guarded PARTS would be a socket that
Rem must be closed on each of a dozen error paths and leaked on the one
Rem that was missed. TRYTCPREQUEST guards the WHOLE conversation
Rem instead — the same call TRYREADFILE makes about whole-file reads,
Rem and for the same reason.
Rem
Rem ---- WHY THIS SEED DOES NOT CALL net.shoddy ------------------------
Rem
Rem It calls TRYTCPREQUEST directly. net.shoddy's own Request is
Rem Connect/Send/RecvAll/Close over the ABORTING builtins, so it is the
Rem thing being replaced rather than the thing being wrapped — exactly
Rem as seedfile.shoddy rebuilds ReadLines rather than calling it.
Rem
Rem AND RECVALL WOULD HANG, which at a prompt is worse than aborting. It
Rem polls until the peer closes, and a keep-alive server never does, so
Rem a session would sit there with no way back. The runtime's read is
Rem bounded by a deadline and gives back what it has.
Rem
Rem ---- WHAT IS NOT BRIDGED ------------------------------------------
Rem
Rem The SERVER half — Listen, Accept, Serve1 — and the raw socket words.
Rem A listener is a live resource with a life between lines, so it would
Rem want the named table; but a server that cannot run a loop is not a
Rem server, and a reckoner line answers and ends. That is a design, and
Rem not this one.
Rem ==================================================================

Include "cuttle.shoddy"
Include "reckoner.shoddy"
Include "seq.shoddy"
Include "str.shoddy"

Rem ---- the two words -----------------------------------------------------

Def NsSeedGet(st As RckState) As RckState
    Let s1 = RckReg(st, "NETGET", 1, RckTouch(Fn(vs) => NsGet(vs)), "( url -- str )", "Fetch a URL and answer the whole reply, headers and all. http:// and https:// both work, and a bare host is taken as https. HTTPBODY, seedhttps's, drops the headers. Refuses, rather than aborting, when the mill has no --allow-net or the host cannot be reached.")
    RckReg(s1, "NETREQUEST", 4, RckTouch(Fn(vs) => NsRequest(vs)), "( host port secure msg -- str )", "Send msg to host on that port, with TLS when secure is True, and answer the whole reply. The raw form NETGET is built on, for a protocol that is not HTTP.")

Rem ---- NETGET ------------------------------------------------------------

Def NsGet(vs As List Of Cell) As RckWork
    Select Case RckTextArg("NETGET", First(vs))
        Case Ok(url)
            NsGetUrl(url)
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad("NETGET did not answer")

Rem A bare host means HTTPS, because a bare host on the modern web means
Rem HTTPS. net.shoddy's own HttpGet speaks plaintext on port 80 and its
Rem header says as much — enough for a demo and not enough for the web.
Def NsGetUrl(url As String) As RckWork
    If StartsWith(Lower(url), "http://") Then
        NsGetParts(Mid(url, 8, Len(url)), False, 80)
    Else
        If StartsWith(Lower(url), "https://") Then
            NsGetParts(Mid(url, 9, Len(url)), True, 443)
        Else
            NsGetParts(url, True, 443)

Rem The host is up to the first slash and the path is the rest, with a
Rem bare host meaning "/". A port written into the host — "example.com:8080"
Rem — is honoured, because a URL may carry one and silently ignoring it
Rem would fetch the wrong thing.
Def NsGetParts(rest As String, secure As Boolean, port As Number) As RckWork
    NsGetSplit(NsUpTo(rest, "/"), NsFrom(rest, "/"), secure, port)

Def NsUpTo(s As String, mark As String) As String
    If Instr(s, mark) = 0 Then
        s
    Else
        Left(s, Instr(s, mark) - 1)

Def NsFrom(s As String, mark As String) As String
    If Instr(s, mark) = 0 Then
        "/"
    Else
        Mid(s, Instr(s, mark), Len(s))

Def NsGetSplit(hostport As String, path As String, secure As Boolean, port As Number) As RckWork
    If Instr(hostport, ":") = 0 Then
        NsGetAt(hostport, hostport, port, secure, path)
    Else
        NsGetPorted(NsUpTo(hostport, ":"), Mid(hostport, Instr(hostport, ":") + 1, Len(hostport)), secure, path)

Def NsGetPorted(host As String, ps As String, secure As Boolean, path As String) As RckWork
    If Not IsNumeric(ps) Then
        RckWorkBad("NETGET: '" & ps & "' IS NOT A PORT NUMBER")
    Else
        NsGetAt(host, host, Val(ps), secure, path)

Rem The Host header carries the name and not the name-with-port, which
Rem is what a server matches its virtual hosts on.
Def NsGetAt(host As String, hdr As String, port As Number, secure As Boolean, path As String) As RckWork
    If host = "" Then
        RckWorkBad("NETGET: THERE IS NO HOST IN THAT URL")
    Else
        NsAsked("NETGET", host, port, secure, NsHttpGet(hdr, path))

Rem HTTP/1.0 with no keep-alive, so the server closes when it is done and
Rem the reply has a definite end. A User-Agent because a good many hosts
Rem refuse a request without one.
Def NsHttpGet(host As String, path As String) As String
    "GET " & path & " HTTP/1.0\r\nHost: " & host & "\r\nUser-Agent: shoddy\r\nConnection: close\r\n\r\n"

Rem ---- NETREQUEST --------------------------------------------------------

Def NsRequest(vs As List Of Cell) As RckWork
    Select Case Pair(RckTextArg("NETREQUEST", Nth(vs, 1)), RckWholeArg("NETREQUEST", Nth(vs, 2)))
        Case Pair(Ok(host), Ok(port))
            NsRequestFlag(host, port, Nth(vs, 3), Nth(vs, 4))
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad("NETREQUEST needs a host and a port")

Def NsRequestFlag(host As String, port As Number, sc As Cell, mc As Cell) As RckWork
    Select Case Pair(sc, mc)
        Case Pair(CutBool(secure), CutStr(msg))
            NsRequestPort(host, port, secure, msg)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad("NETREQUEST needs True or False for secure, and a STRING to send")

Rem A port outside 1..65535 is not a port. The runtime would report the
Rem failure anyway, but it would report it as a connection that could not
Rem be made, which is a worse account of a number that was mistyped.
Def NsRequestPort(host As String, port As Number, secure As Boolean, msg As String) As RckWork
    If port < 1 Or port > 65535 Then
        RckWorkBad("NETREQUEST: " & Str(port) & " IS NOT A PORT — THEY RUN FROM 1 TO 65535")
    Else
        NsAsked("NETREQUEST", host, port, secure, msg)

Rem ---- the one place either of them touches the network -------------------

Rem NETALLOWED is asked FIRST, so a mill with no --allow-net is told what
Rem is actually wrong rather than being handed a failure that reads like
Rem the host's fault. The runtime reports it too; this one names the
Rem remedy, which the runtime is not the place to do.
Def NsAsked(word As String, host As String, port As Number, secure As Boolean, msg As String) As RckWork
    If Not NetAllowed() Then
        RckWorkBad(word & ": THE NETWORK IS OFF — START THE MILL WITH --allow-net")
    Else
        NsAnswered(word, TryTcpRequest(host, port, secure, msg))

Def NsAnswered(word As String, r As Result) As RckWork
    Select Case r
        Case Ok(reply)
            RckVals({ CutStr(reply) })
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(word & ": " & w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad(word & " did not answer")

Rem THERE WAS A NETBODY HERE, and seedhttps.shoddy is where it went. It
Rem split a reply at the blank line, which is what HTTPBODY does — and
Rem HTTPBODY is https.shoddy's own, so it does it properly. Keeping both
Rem would have been a second spelling of a word the dictionary already
Rem had, which is the thing seedbuiltin's own rules exist to prevent. A
Rem mill that wants replies taken apart folds seedhttps; this seed
Rem fetches the text.

Rem ---- the seed ----------------------------------------------------------

Def RckSeedNet(st As RckState) As RckState
    NsSeedGet(RckSeeding(st, "seed-net"))
